The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (snd_seq_write_pool_allocated(client)) {
    /* remove all existing cells */
    snd_seq_pool_mark_closing(client->pool);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the pool can be resized while it is in use, leading to the deletion of existing cells. If another thread is concurrently writing to the pool during this operation, it can result in a use-after-free (UAF) situation, causing undefined behavior or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (atomic_read(&client->pool->counter))
    return -EBUSY;
```
Reason:
This code checks if the pool is currently in use by reading the atomic counter. If the counter is greater than zero, it indicates that the pool is in use, and the function returns an error (-EBUSY), preventing the resizing operation and thus avoiding the race condition and potential UAF.

